---
description: Decompose a rough idea into a sequence of discrete features<% if (it.isSdd) { %> and walk every feature to an approved spec<% } %> (epic-level brainstorm).
---

Acting as the `leader`, help the human turn a rough idea into a sequence of small, shippable features. The idea is in `$ARGUMENTS` — if it is empty, ask the human to describe what they want to build before going further. You are **planning only**: you do not write product code here, and you do not touch `feature_list.json` until the human approves.

1. **Understand the idea.** Read `AGENTS.md` and skim the relevant parts of the codebase. Ask the human any clarifying questions you need. For a large or unfamiliar area, delegate to explorer subagents so the breakdown is grounded in what actually exists — don't guess the architecture. Explorers are read-only and disposable: launch them cheap (low effort) — their output is summarized findings, not code.

2. **Decompose.** Break the idea into discrete features, each small enough to flow through the harness one at a time. Choose an `<epic-slug>` for the whole idea and a `slug` for each feature — all matching `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$`. For every feature capture a one-line title, a one-line rationale, its `dependsOn` (other slugs in this batch), where it sits in the recommended order, and its **lane** — run `npx reins route "<feature title>" --json` for each (quick / chore / standard / full) and keep or override its answer. Lanes decide how much process each feature gets: splitting an epic should not give a one-line tweak the ceremony of an auth rewrite. An **upgrade** (SDK, framework, major dependency) is **one** `chore` feature — bump, breaking-change fixes, and verification together; splitting it into bump / fix / verification features multiplies native builds and reviews.

3. **Write the breakdown to disk** at `progress/brainstorm_<epic-slug>.md` *before* you stop, so the proposal survives even if the conversation is later compacted. Use this shape:

   ```
   # Brainstorm — <epic-slug>

   ## Idea
   <the human's idea, as you understand it>

   ## Proposed features
   | # | slug | title | lane | depends on | why |
   | - | ---- | ----- | ---- | ---------- | --- |
   | 1 | <slug> | <title> | standard | — | <rationale> |
   | 2 | <slug> | <title> | quick | <slug> | <rationale> |

   ## Open questions
   - <anything the human still needs to decide>

   ## Resolution
   <filled in after approval: the features actually registered>
   ```

4. **Present the breakdown and stop.** Show the human the proposed features and the order, and **wait for their approval in chat**. If they want changes, revise the list and the file. Register nothing yet.

5. **On approval, register the features** as `pending`, in dependency-first order (a feature's dependencies before the feature itself), by running for each one:

   ```
   reins add-feature <slug> --title "<title>" --lane <lane> --depends-on <a,b> --json
   ```

   Do **not** pass `--with-spec`. The command dedupes by slug, so re-running is safe; report which features were added and which were skipped.
<% if (it.isSdd) { %>
6. **Spec pipeline — run it now, in parallel, with one round of questions.** This is where the human settles every detail, so that implementation later needs **no further questions or approvals**. Work by lane:

   - **`quick` / `chore` features** need nothing here — they stay `pending` and are ready for `/next-feature` (or `/autopilot`) as soon as their dependencies are `done`.
   - **Discovery, all at once.** Set every `standard` and `full` feature `analyzing` (several may be analyzing at once — only `in_progress` is limited to one). Delegate to their explorers **concurrently**, then write each feature's document: `specs/<slug>/plan.md` (copied from `specs/_template/plan.md`) for `standard`, `specs/<slug>/discovery.md` for `full`. Set `standard` features `spec_ready` and `full` features `needs_clarification`.
   - **One round of questions.** Present **every** open question from every feature in a **single message**, grouped by feature, with the standard plans summarized alongside. Wait for the human's answers, and record them in each document's **Resolution** (adjusting any plan an answer changes).
   - **Specs, in parallel.** For the `full` features, delegate to `spec_author` for each — concurrently, since each writes only its own `specs/<slug>/` — grounded in the resolved discovery, never the bare title. Each sets its feature `spec_ready`.
   - **One approval round.** Present a short summary of every plan and spec together and ask for approval — the human may approve all, or name the features to revise. Set each approved feature `approved`; revise and re-present the rest. Never advance an unapproved feature.

   If this is interrupted, resume each feature from its current state (`analyzing`/`needs_clarification` → finish the Q&A; `spec_ready` → present for approval), or use `/validate-discovery <feature>` (full lane) and `/approve-spec <feature>` individually.

7. **Close the loop.** Record the final feature list in the **Resolution** section of `progress/brainstorm_<epic-slug>.md`, run `<%= it.verifyCmd %>` to confirm a green tree, then tell the human: every `standard`/`full` feature is `approved` and every `quick`/`chore` feature is ready — from here, `/next-feature` (or `/autopilot`) implements them one at a time with **no further questions or approvals**.

Remember: brainstorm registers features and carries each `standard`/`full` one to `approved` — it never sets a feature `in_progress`, and all human questioning belongs here, not during implementation. Write every file you save (`progress/brainstorm_*.md`, plans, discoveries, specs) in English, even if this conversation is in another language.
<% } else { %>
6. **Close the loop.** Record the final feature list in the **Resolution** section of `progress/brainstorm_<epic-slug>.md`, run `<%= it.verifyCmd %>` to confirm a green tree, then tell the human to start work with `/next-feature` (or `/autopilot`). Each feature will be implemented directly, one at a time, with the review its lane calls for.

Remember: brainstorm only ever creates `pending` features — it never sets a feature `in_progress`. Write every file you save (`progress/brainstorm_*.md` and any other artifact) in English, even if this conversation is in another language.
<% } %>